<article class="env-corollary" id="03Y6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03Y6">Corollary <span data-tag="03Y6">8.1.2.18</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an uncountable cardinal and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is essentially $\kappa $-small, then $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is essentially $\kappa $-small. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose an equivalence of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}'$ is a $\kappa $-small simplicial set. It follows from Corollary <a data-tag="03K9" href="/tag/03K9">8.1.2.17</a> that the induced map $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}') \rightarrow \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is also an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. We conclude by observing that $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}')$ is also a $\kappa $-small simplicial set (Remark <a data-tag="03Y5" href="/tag/03Y5">8.1.1.5</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
